# OCR in the Cloud

**Ammar Mawassi** · Student ID `213620586`

## User

My local client reads one image URL per line and uploads the file to S3.
It checks EC2 tags for a ready Manager with a free slot and starts one if
needed. If the Manager rejects the job, the client tries another one.
If a reply is delayed, it retries the same Manager with the same workID.
I chose a temporary SQS reply queue per workID so each client gets its own
replies. The client downloads the summary and writes the HTML in input
order. Each image links to its original URL. Failed images show the URL
and a short error.

## Manager

I gave each Manager three queues for client requests, image tasks, and
Worker results. One thread checks capacity and dispatches tasks in small
batches. The other records results and publishes completed summaries.
This lets the Manager accept work while results are still coming in.
The Manager checks capacity again on admission because two clients can
see the same free slot. It saves the job before dispatching image tasks
and sends the client a reply after saving the summary to S3.

## Worker

Each Worker pulls from its Manager's shared task queue, downloads the
image, follows redirects, and runs OCR. It returns the jobID and the text
or error. I chose one image at a time per Worker to limit memory use.
Workers can process images in parallel across EC2 instances. I used
Tess4J on Windows and the packaged Tesseract executable on EC2 because
Tess4J had a native library mismatch on the Ubuntu image.

## Queues and data flow

```text
User -> request queue -> Manager -> task queue -> Workers
Workers -> result queue -> Manager -> temporary reply queue -> User
```

S3 holds the input, progress, OCR text, and final summaries.

## Scaling and load balancing

I divide each active job's unfinished distinct URL count by its worker-files-n,
add the shares, and round up. I request one Worker as the Manager starts, then adjust the target on a background thread while the request thread sends tasks. Starting and running Workers both count
toward that target. Workers take the next task as they become free, so a
slow image ties up only one Worker. The client waits for its own summary
while other jobs can keep moving. The lab instance cap limits throughput.
At much larger scale I would partition work across more Manager pools
and use admission backpressure to keep the backlog manageable.

## Persistence and failure recovery

A jobID combines the workID and the URL's first input line number. I send one
task per distinct URL in a job and reuse its result for each matching input
row. If a URL appears twice, it gets one task and two report rows.
The official sample has 10 distinct URLs and 24 report rows. The Manager counts an SQS redelivery only once. The Worker extends
visibility during OCR and deletes a task after
publishing its result. If it dies, another Worker can pick up the task
after the timeout. I save progress in S3 so a Manager restarted with the
same ID can reload completed results and dispatch unfinished tasks.

## Security and termination

The client uses my temporary Learner Lab credentials from the local AWS
profile. EC2 uses an IAM instance profile, and the S3 bucket stays private.
The JAR, settings file, and startup script contain no credentials.
With `terminate`, the Manager enters `DRAINING`, rejects new jobs, and finishes
accepted jobs before removing its queues and terminating its Workers and
itself. The client removes its temporary reply queue.

## Build and run

Use Java 17 and Maven. Build with `mvn clean package` and copy
`target/ocr-cloud-1.0-SNAPSHOT.jar` to `yourjar.jar`. From the folder with
the JAR and the course sample input, run

```powershell
java -jar yourjar.jar input-example.txt output.html 2 12 terminate
```

The numeric arguments are Manager client capacity and files per Worker.
The `terminate` argument is optional.

Start the Learner Lab and save its temporary credentials under learnerlab
in your local AWS credentials file. Grader-Test is configured for my lab.
For another lab, set AWS_PROFILE, AWS_REGION, HW1_BUCKET, HW1_PREFIX,
HW1_AMI, and HW1_INSTANCE_PROFILE in `hw1.properties` or the shell. Keep
HW1_PREFIX at 20 letters, digits, or hyphens maximum. Set HW1_SUBNET and
HW1_SECURITY_GROUP if needed. Set HW1_INSTANCE_LIMIT to your lab limit
if it is below 19.

Use an image with Java 17, AWS CLI, curl, and Tesseract installed, or set
HW1_BOOT_INSTALL=true on Ubuntu x86-64. Upload yourjar.jar as ocr-cloud.jar
along with target/ocr-worker.jar and eng.traineddata under <HW1_PREFIX>/artifacts in the private bucket.
HW1_ARTIFACT_PREFIX can override that path. Get the language file from
https://github.com/tesseract-ocr/tessdata_fast/blob/main/eng.traineddata

## AWS sample run and tests

| Setting | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Region | `us-east-1` |
| AMI | Private Ubuntu 24.04 runtime image (`ami-0ab28d387d74b3e64`) |
| Instances | One `t3.micro` Manager and one `t2.micro` Worker |
| Parameters | Manager capacity 2, files per Worker 12 |
| Client runtime | 72.6 seconds from client start to exit |
| Sample output | [output-aws.html](output-aws.html), 24 rows with 12 OCR results, nine HTTP 404s, and three DNS failures |

I chose `t3.micro` for the Manager's two threads and `t2.micro` for Workers
after package installation was killed during startup on a `t2.nano`.
The private AMI has Java 17, AWS CLI, and Tesseract installed. EC2 fetches its JAR from S3 at launch. Workers use a smaller JAR with only the runtime they need. The Grader-Test settings select this AMI and skip package installation.

### Startup benchmark

I start one Worker as the Manager comes up and provision additional Workers
in the background while sending tasks. The prebuilt AMI skips package
installation, and I reduced the Worker JAR from 71.7 MiB to 19.1 MiB.
I added timestamps for launch, artifact download, Java readiness, and the
first task.

| Cold run | Workers | Files per Worker | Client runtime |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Before the startup changes | 1 | 12 | 109.9 s |
| After the startup changes | 1 | 12 | 72.6 s |
| After the startup changes | 2 | 5 | 84.3 s |

The one-Worker run was 37.3 seconds faster (34%) with the same AMI and
input. This comparison measures the startup changes together. In the
two-Worker run, artifact downloads took about 4–6 seconds per Worker.

### Other AWS checks

I ran two six-image clients together and checked that each got its own
ordered report. With capacity 1, the second client launched a separate
Manager. A last-slot test returned one acceptance and one rejection.
Mixed ratios of 3 and 6 launched three Workers for two six-image jobs.
I verified Worker redelivery in the fetch log and the completed S3 summary
after the client-side test wait expired. I restarted a draining Manager
with three of six results saved and verified the full ordered summary.
I also checked that draining rejected new work and that the test instances
and queues were cleaned up.
